                  <provenance type="#c" cert="low"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_86">Bury St Edmunds,
                     Suffolk. Benedictine abbey of St Edmund, King and Martyr</orgName><p>One quire
                     only, bound with a 16th-cent. <q>Vita et passio s. Edmundi . . . transcripta ex
                     vetusto exemplari MSto cum libro Buriensi colligato</q>, and two leaves (also
                     16th-c. paper) with annals of St Augustine's abbey, Canterbury. Assigned to
                     Bury by M. R. James (<title>Abbey</title> of St Edmund, 87 no.
                     267).</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_122">Canterbury, Kent,
                     Benedictine abbey of St Augustine (originally of St Peter and St
                     Paul)</orgName><p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of
                     Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the
                     Supplement (1987). The corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index
                     card gives the following context for the rejection: W. H. Frere,
                     <title>Bibliotheca musico-liturgica</title>, I, no. 11 says <q>probably from St
                     Augustine's Canterbury</q>, but gives no reasons.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">John Lumley</persName>,
                     first Baron Lumley (c. 1533–1609).</provenance>
